{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "handling_time_gap",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "issue_type",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Issue type plausibly affects which channel/priority a ticket takes (e.g., outages routed to fast synchronous channels, high priority) AND independently affects handling time (complexity). It is not named in the query and is a genuine common cause, making it the strongest evidence of surfaced causal reasoning."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_async_channel",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "This is a re-encoding of channel, one of the two candidate causes explicitly named in the query whose effect on handling time is being attributed."
    },
    {
      "name": "priority_numeric",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "A numeric re-encoding of priority, the other candidate cause explicitly named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "expected_resolution_minutes",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A workload/SLA-based expectation baseline that plausibly reflects underlying case difficulty/SLA tier, which can influence both channel/priority assignment and actual handling time. Not named in the query. Somewhat ambiguous because it may be derived from priority itself, weakening its independence."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_deviation_pct",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Deviation of actual from expected resolution time is essentially an operationalization of the handling_time_gap outcome, not a confounder; adjusting for it would be adjusting for the outcome."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["is_async_channel", "priority_numeric"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "issue_type", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.85},
    {"name": "expected_resolution_minutes", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.45}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.55,
  "rationale": "Treatments (channel via is_async_channel, priority via priority_numeric) are present but both are merely re-encodings of variables explicitly named in the query, so they add no new causal insight. The decisive capability is confounder surfacing. issue_type is a genuine, not-named common cause of both treatment (routing/prioritization) and outcome (handling time), demonstrating real causal reasoning. expected_resolution_minutes is a weaker, partly derived candidate that may be a mediator/re-expression of priority rather than an independent confounder. resolution_deviation_pct is effectively the outcome itself and must not be treated as a confounder. Overall there is one solid surfaced confounder plus one ambiguous one, so confounder quality is moderate."
}

